AllocationSummary:
The Regional Humanitarian Pooled Fund for Asia and the Pacific (RHPF-AP) – Bangladesh envelope seeks US$150 million to support life-saving humanitarian assistance under the 2025–2026 Joint Response Plan (JRP) for the Rohingya Humanitarian Crisis and address emerging needs from natural disasters.

Bangladesh hosts over one million Rohingya refugees alongside vulnerable host communities in Cox’s Bazar, one of the country’s poorest and most disaster-prone districts. Continued displacement from Myanmar, including 130,000 new arrivals in 2025 and 35,000 expected in 2026, has intensified humanitarian needs. The 2026 JRP targets 1.56 million people, with financial requirements of US$698.4 million, including US$495.3 million in Priority 1 life-saving interventions. Funding shortfalls have heightened food insecurity, malnutrition, protection risks, and exposure to climate-related hazards.

Bangladesh also faces recurrent climate shocks—cyclones, floods, landslides, and disease outbreaks—affecting millions annually. Between 2015 and 2024, disasters affected an average of 9.7 million people per year. In 2024 alone, Cyclone Remal and widespread flooding affected 18 million people, compounding vulnerabilities in refugee-hosting areas.

Managed by OCHA’s Regional Office for Asia and the Pacific (ROAP) under the Resident Coordinator (RC), the RHPF-AP Bangladesh envelope provides flexible, timely financing to priority sectors identified through coordinated humanitarian processes. Allocations are determined through a transparent, consultative mechanism involving an Advisory Board of local and international NGOs, UN agencies, cluster coordinators, donors, and government counterparts. Contributions are unearmarked and allocated based on agreed priorities.

The Fund operates through two windows: (i) Standard Allocations aligned with JRP priorities, and (ii) Reserve Allocations for rapid response to unforeseen emergencies. In 2026, the Fund will focus on acute food insecurity, malnutrition, inadequate WASH services, shelter and NFI gaps, health needs, protection risks including GBV and child protection, and preparedness for climate hazards. Key priorities include:

General food assistance and fresh food vouchers, with LPG support for safe cooking;

Prevention and treatment of acute malnutrition among children under five and pregnant and breastfeeding women;

Emergency WASH, health services, and support to field hospitals;

Emergency shelter and NFI assistance;

Protection services for individuals at heightened risk.

The RHPF-AP emphasizes localization, prioritizing national and local partners and promoting humanitarian action that is “as local as possible, as international as necessary.” Consolidated regional management ensures cost-efficiency and compliance with OCHA’s Global Guidelines for CBPFs.

Monitoring and accountability are central. A risk-based approach prioritizes high-risk projects, with field visits, third-party monitoring where required, and remote monitoring in access-constrained areas. Robust due diligence, financial oversight, reporting, audits, and UN oversight ensure transparency. The Fund upholds strong Accountability to Affected Populations (AAP) standards, including feedback mechanisms and strict compliance with the UN’s zero-tolerance policy on Sexual Exploitation and Abuse (SEA).

Through predictable, flexible, and principled pooled financing, the RHPF-AP Bangladesh envelope strengthens coordinated humanitarian leadership, addresses funding gaps, empowers local actors, and enables timely, life-saving assistance to the most vulnerable refugees and host communities.

Cette allocation s’inscrit dans la vision 2025 du Coordonnateur Humanitaire (CH) de la RCA et de la communauté humanitaire du pays, en combinant continuité opérationnelle, localisation, réponse rapide, approche sensible au genre et redevabilité. Elle vise également à renforcer les capacités des acteurs locaux, en particulier les organisations dirigées par des femmes (WLO), tout en maintenant la possibilité de répondre rapidement aux alertes humanitaires.   

 

Les WLO, grâce à leur ancrage communautaire et leur présence dans les zones difficiles d’accès, jouent un rôle clé pour assurer la continuité de l’assistance et des réponses sensibles au genre. Cependant, leur accès limité aux financements directs (en raison de contraintes institutionnelles ou techniques) freine souvent leur potentiel. 

 

Pour soutenir des réponses humanitaires pertinentes tout en consolidant la localisation et en renforçant le leadership des organisations féminines, le Coordonnateur Humanitaire, après consultation du Comité Consultatif du FH RCA, lance une allocation de $8 millions (dont un minimum de 45% directement pour les ONG Nationales incluant les WLO), visant à : 

Maintenir une capacité opérationnelle dans les zones prioritaires où les partenaires restent disponibles et pertinents ; 

Financer directement des réponses menées par des WLO, notamment dans les zones où l’accès est limité ; 

Renforcer les capacités institutionnelles et techniques des WLO, y compris via des projets portés par d’autres acteurs ; 

Prioriser les zones ayant généré le plus grand nombre d’alertes humanitaires et touchant le plus grand nombre de personnes au cours des 12 derniers mois ; 

Préserver une capacité de réponse rapide, permettant de traiter les besoins déjà identifiés ainsi que les nouvelles alertes. 

 

Cette approche permettra de maintenir la présence humanitaire, de renforcer le leadership des WLO et d’assurer une allocation flexible et réactive, tout en contribuant à l’agenda de localisation et à la protection des populations.

En étroite collaboration avec le Conseil Consultatif (CC) du FHRDC, le Coordonnateur Humanitaire (CH) a approuvé une Allocation de réserve (AR) de US $2.23 millions visant à fournir une plate-forme de coordination plus solide pour informer une prise de décision stratégique du Coordonnateur Humanitaire (CH) de l’assistance humanitaire des populations les plus vulnérables.
 
La continuité du renforcement de la capacité de la coordination de la réponse humanitaire en RDC à travers des représentants neutres des clusters est nécessaire afin de capitaliser sur les acquis du financement initié par le financement du FH RDC en 2018 et poursuivre les efforts entamés afin de : 
1.	Renforcer significativement les capacités de co-facilitation des clusters en vue d’améliorer la coordination de la réponse humanitaire au niveau national et dans les pôles régionaux ; 
2.	Améliorer l'analyse des données et la gestion de l'information pour une meilleure planification de la réponse humanitaire ;

Cette allocation permettra de :
1) Garantir une assistante d’urgence aux nouveaux déplacés en Abris, Articles ménagers essentiels et en Sécurité alimentaire ;
2) Faciliter le déploiement de l’assistance en temps réel dans les provinces de l’Ituri et du Nord-Kivu à partir des lieux de stockage à Bunia et Bénin en fonction des besoins.

Depuis janvier 2025, la dégradation accélérée de la situation sécuritaire à l’Est de la République Démocratique du Congo, marquée par la prise stratégique des principales villes par le M23, comme Goma, et Bukavu, a provoqué le déplacement de plus de 850 000 personnes. Parallèlement, la fermeture forcée des sites de déplacés autour de Goma a entraîné des retours précipités, souvent non volontaires, aggravant la vulnérabilité des populations déjà fragilisées par des décennies de crise. En Ituri, l’escalade des violences des groupes armées et des tensions intercommunautaires a généré plus de 108 700 nouveaux déplacements, exacerbant une situation humanitaire déjà critique.
C’est dans ce contexte que cette Première Allocation de Réserve 2025 du Fonds Humanitaire en RDC (FH RDC), d’un montant de 20 millions USD a été validée avec une enveloppe répartie comme suit : Nord Kivu (7 millions de USD) ; Sud Kivu (6 millions) et Ituri (7 Millions) afin de fournir les ressources permettant d’adresser les priorités suivantes :
•	Contribuer à une mise à l’échelle de la réponse humanitaire en adressant les besoins vitaux des populations les plus vulnérables (personnes déplacées, personnes retournées et communautés hôtes) affectées par les derniers développements sécuritaires dans les provinces de l’Ituri, du Nord et du Sud-Kivu, consécutive à l’activisme du M23 et des autres groupes armés ; 
•	Prendre en compte les zones non-couvertes par les financements actuels, y compris la récente allocation CERF Rapid Response 2025 de 17 millions USD en adressant les besoins les plus critiques des populations affectées par la crise ;
•	Renforcer la résilience des populations touchées par la crise, avec une attention particulière sur les populations déplacées internes et les retournés en fournissant un appui multisectoriel visant à faciliter leur réintégration progressive et de façon durable dans les communautés hôtes ou d’origine.

Cette troisième allocation de réserve de 3 millions USD initiée par le Coordonnateur Humanitaire cible les Zones de santé  de Kalemie et Nyemba en Territoire de Kalemie et la Zone de Santé de Kansimba en territoire de Moba. Elle vise à renforcer l’impact global de la réponse humanitaire dans le Tanganyika en combinant une approche géographique focalisée et une intervention multisectorielle sur les priorités identifiées : : 
•	Répondre à l’épidémie de choléra à travers l’appui à la prise en charge médicale et le renforcement des mécanismes communautaires de prévention et de lutte ;
•	Renforcer la sécurité alimentaire par des activités de production agricole d’urgence ;
•	Améliorer l’accès aux soins de santé, en particulier l’assistance nutritionnelle ;
•	Mettre en place des mécanismes de protection pour atténuer les risques et assurer la prise en charge des victimes de violations des droits humains ; 
•	Fournir un accès immédiat aux articles ménagers essentiels (AME).

It is the first reserve allocation organized by the Syria Cross-border Humanitarian Fund to support cross-border actors operating in North East Syria. The principal purpose of this reserve allocation is to support and maintain the emergency response in most affected areas as well as support critical activities in hosting locations, accessible directly cross-border from Iraq and where the response from within Syria has been limited, with an emphasis on the following strategic objectives:

•	Assisting the newly displaced, particularly those in collective centres, camps and residing with friends/ family in host communities in Hassakeh and Raqqa governorates:
o	Providing RTEs to newly displaced households and eventually general food rations/ cash for food for people facing longer-term displacement as well as kitchen kitsm;
o	Providing winterization assistance including winter kits, heaters/ fuel and access to adequate shelters.
o	Establishing health services including through static capacity in new/ reactivated camps and mobile medical unit (MMUs) for IDPs in collective centres;
o	Ensuring sustained delivery of safe/ potable drinking water to IDPs / host communities, particularly in areas where supplies are under considerable strain’;
o	Expanding access to adequate and dignified sanitation, through latrine installation, rehabilitation (in collective centres) and maintenance;
o	Providing psycho-social support and psychological first aid to newly displaced persons. 
•	Enhancing support to host communities, and areas of returnees particularly in relation to the delivery of basic services such as health, water and sanitation and waste management:
o	Maintain support to health facilities and delivery points, including frontline hospitals and some critical Primary Health Care facilities (PHCs),;
o	Provide mine risk education for people living in contaminated areas and scale-up mine clearance operations in Raqqa city and surrounding areas where operations have faced severe disruptions;
o	Support urgent school repair activities as part of winterization efforts - ensuring children have access to safe and productive learning environments;
o	Enhance child protection interventions for vulnerable children through distribution of PSS kits, awareness raising activities (including safe identification and referral) and provision of recreational activities;
•	Expanding partnerships and engaging in capacity building as part of individual NGO strategies to ensure continuity of response and enable a shift in modality should partners lose access or international staff presence be reduced.

This reserve allocation will be focused on the provision of assistance through cross-border operations from Iraq, ensuring complementarity with other response modalities focusing on a) specific sectoral emergency activities which are critical and either not met or not able to be fully addressed by other response modalities e.g. ERW clearance, sustained support to medical facilities, provision of heaters and fuel b) geographic locations where NGO partners have greater proximity and ease of operating e.g. newly established camps, c) areas and camps where there remain unmet needs despite joint effort from all modalities especially in health, WASH, protection and other winterization efforts in all other camps where gaps have been identified e.g. Deir Ez Zor, Raqqa and Menbij Programmatically, the allocation will prioritize the provision of emergency multi-sectoral assistance to new IDPs as well as support to maintaining on-going response given reprioritization and redirection which has resulted from the emergency - including, winterization assistance, support to basic healthcare provision and protection, including mine risk education as well as mine clearance and, relatedly, school repairs for winter preparedness and urgent restoration of safe learning environments.
•	Under each strategic objective the projects must have a strong emphasis on:
o	Wome

This emergency allocation will address the immediate needs as outlined below and, considering the very volatile environment, aims to be flexible in responding to the immediate and urgent needs on the ground for relocation, newly displaced persons and scale-up services in camps and overburdened host communities. Interventions will aim to scale up and complement the ongoing response, including ongoing reprogramming of existing interventions that have been affected or interrupted by the ongoing violence. This strategy has been developed based on the most recent figures and numbers and locations in the readiness and response plan and will be updated during proposals as the situation rapidly evolves.  
As such, the allocation will align with the three objectives of the humanitarian readiness and response plan:
1.	Allocation Strategic Objective (ASO) 1: Protection of civilians
	Protection services: will be implemented through an emergency response model that relies on expanding the capacity of static service points (community centers, child-friendly spaces, and women and girls’ safe spaces) and mobile outreach teams. Due to the dynamic nature of the Syrian context, this approach supports affected populations with multiple entry points to protection services and allows cluster members to continue the delivery of services in rapidly-changing circumstances to respond to existing gaps and anticipated growing needs. Procurement and provision of dignity kits are also included;
	Safe Relocation: of people to save areas by provision of transportation services. This includes relocation support and facilitation of routes for people to move safely away from hostilities and to safe destinations.
2.	Allocation Strategic Objective (ASO) 2: Provision of safe reception centers and shelters in and out of camps 
	CCCM and emergency shelter: Expansion of existing and establishment of new camps to increase emergency shelter capacity for the newly displaced IDPs, provision of camp management and coordination in the newly established and extended camps and provision of protection and mainstreamed multi-sectoral lifesaving assistance in these sites  and procurement of emergency shelter; 
	WASH: Procurement and provision of critical Water Sanitation and Hygiene (WASH) supplies and services to people in newly established and extended camp and out of camp locations  with priority to locations that are overwhelmed with new arrivals;
3.	Allocation Strategic Objective (ASO) 3: Provision of emergency services in and out of camps;
	Food assistance and livelihoods: Prepositioning and distributing of ready-to-eat-rations (RTER), food baskets, support to mobile kitchens and cooked meals to respond to the basic needs of newly displaced IDPs;  
	Health: Procurement and provision of medical supplies, including trauma and surgical kits/supplies, anesthetics drugs and supplementary medicines, support to ambulance services, mobile clinics in IDPs sites and facilities in targeted locations that are overwhelmed with new arrivals;
	Non-Food Items (NFIs) and emergency shelter: Procurement and provision of core pipelines (Non-Food Items (NFIs) and shelter materials that are considered indispensable for the delivery of the frontline response; 
	Common Services: increased logistic support to humanitarian partners through coordinating transshipment services and delivery of humanitarian cargo from Turkey into northwest Syria via the Bab Al-Hawa and Bab Al-Salam border crossings; 
	Nutrition: scale-up of nutrition services to respond to increased needs where acute malnutrition of children 6-59 months has almost double one out of each three mothers and children in NWS is acutely malnourished and /or potentially anemic.
